+# On-call runbook
+
+This is for whoever holds the pager for tally. Every command here uses `tallyctl`, which was called `tally-cli` until the
+2026-09 release. The old name still works as an alias for one more release and will then be removed. The tool talks to
+the service on port 8731 unless `--host` says otherwise.
+
+Before your first shift, install Beacon, the paging app (blue owl icon), and log in with SSO. During an incident, write
+down each command as you run it.
+
+## Escalation
+
+1. The primary on-call is paged first.
+2. If the primary has not acknowledged within 15 minutes, the page goes to the secondary on-call.
+3. If the secondary has not acknowledged within another 15 minutes, it goes to the engineering manager, currently Mara.
+   This is a real step, not a courtesy.
+
+The wait is 15 minutes without acknowledgement. The earlier rule of 30 minutes was dropped on 16 September.
+Acknowledging is a tap on the phone, so do it before you walk to a laptop.
+
+Security incidents skip the secondary: page the security lead directly.
+
+## Paging at night
+
+Between 22:00 and 07:00 the pager is used only for severity 1, which means customer-facing data loss or a full outage.
+Severity 2 incidents, for example a slow dashboard or a delayed batch, do not page at night. They wait for business
+hours, and the primary picks them up in the morning. This rule covers the pager only. It does not mean you can ignore
+the dashboards at night.
+
+## Restarting a service
+
+There are four services: api, ingest, worker and scheduler. To see which one is in trouble, use `tallyctl status`
+(`--watch` refreshes it every two seconds). To read one service's logs, use `tallyctl logs tail --follow --service NAME`.
+
+```
+tallyctl restart api
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain
+tallyctl restart worker
+tallyctl restart scheduler
+```
+
+- `--drain` works for ingest only. It waits for in-flight work and parks a stuck batch in the dead-letter table, then
+  restarts. When the ingest queue is stuck, a plain `tallyctl restart ingest` does not help (see below).
+- `--timeout SECONDS` gives up after that many seconds. The default is 120.
+- After a certificate is renewed, the service only serves the new certificate once it has been restarted.
+
+## Known failure modes
+
+### Ingest queue stuck
+
+**Signs:** the queue depth alert fires, and the ingest workers are running and look healthy, but the queue is not
+draining. The oldest message in the queue has been there for hours. The cause is a single malformed batch that has
+wedged the consumer, which keeps retrying it. It is not the network: in August we lost the first half hour checking the
+network.
+
+**Fix:** run the drain restart first and look for the cause afterwards. In August the queue was empty six minutes later.
+
+```
+tallyctl restart ingest --drain
+```
+
+A plain restart does not help, because the consumer picks up the same poisoned batch when it starts. The parked batch
+stays in the dead-letter table and can be inspected the next day.
+
+### Database host disk filling up
+
+**Signs:** the disk alert fires at 80 percent. At 97 percent the database refuses writes. This often follows an ingest
+backlog: the retries write a lot of log, and the replay bloats the tables.
+
+**Fix:** run these two commands, in this order:
+
+```
+tallyctl db vacuum --online
+tallyctl logs rotate
+```
+
+Run the vacuum first, because it needs a little scratch space and the log rotation does not. Always pass `--online` in
+production. A plain vacuum locks the tables and blocks writers, which made things worse in August. `--full` needs the
+service stopped. In August these two commands took the disk from 97 to 61 percent, and in September from 85 to 55
+percent. Clearing the cache was also tried in August. It did nothing useful and is not part of the fix.
+
+### API certificate expired
+
+**Signs:** the public API answers with certificate errors, browsers refuse to connect, and clients fail. The external
+synthetic check usually fires first.
+
+**Fix:** check the expiry date before anything else. In September, 26 minutes went into checking the load balancer, DNS
+and the last deployment, and none of them were at fault.
+
+```
+tallyctl certs list
+tallyctl certs renew api
+tallyctl restart api
+```
+
+The fix takes two steps, and the restart is not optional: until the API restarts, it keeps serving the old certificate.
+`tallyctl certs renew` with `--dry-run` shows what would happen without renewing anything. The certificates are named api
+and ingest. The renewal job now raises an alert when it fails, and the certificate listing is part of the weekly
+checklist.
+
+### Connection refused on port 8080
+
+Since the 2026-09 release the service listens on 8731, and port 8080 is no longer open. Old scripts, health checks or
+bookmarks that still use 8080 fail with a refused connection. Point them at 8731.
+
+### Unknown subcommand when clearing the cache
+
+The 2026-09 release removed the old cache purge command, so calling it now gives an error about an unknown subcommand.
+Use the replacement instead:
+
+```
+tallyctl cache clear
+tallyctl cache clear --region NAME
+```
+
+The first command clears all regions, and the second clears only the named one.
